RBAC Middleware

AS-IS
	•	Authentication (API Key):
Works as API Key Mocked (no API Key generation/verification).
	•	Authorization (JWT/JWKS):
No JWT token or session manager (no JWT issuer/JWKS verifier).
Access control is mocked with hard-coded roles (admin/operator) instead of being managed in a database schema.
No access control is implemented for ContractAddress or WalletAddress (no allowlist).

⸻

TO-BE: Option 1 (Middleware)
	•	Authentication (API Key):
Implement an API Key generator and verifier to manage the full API Key lifecycle as part of the database schema.
	•	Authorization (JWT/JWKS):
Define a JWT token manager with a configurable TTL (time-to-live).
Database schema should define Users, Roles, Permissions, etc.
Consider operation-level access control for ContractAddress, WalletAddress, and other necessary attributes.
Use a cache manager to store JWTs, JWKS, and permissions.

⸻

TO-BE: Option 2 (API Gateway + Middleware)
	•	Authentication (API Key):
API Gateway is responsible for full API Key lifecycle management (issuer/verifier).
	•	Authorization (JWT/JWKS):
Middleware defines a JWT token manager with configurable TTL.
Database schema defines Users, Roles, Permissions, etc.
Middleware enforces operation-level access control for ContractAddress, WalletAddress, and others.
Use a cache manager to store JWTs, JWKS, and permissions.

⸻

TO-BE: Option 3 (API Gateway + IdP + Middleware)
	•	Authentication (API Key):
API Gateway is responsible for API Key lifecycle management (issuer/verifier).
	•	Authorization (JWT/JWKS):
Identity Provider (IdP) is responsible for authentication and authorization via JWT lifecycle automation (issuer + JWKS verifier).
Minimal custom schema can be supported in the IdP.
Middleware can enforce additional operation-level access control (ContractAddress, WalletAddress, etc., if necessary).
Use a cache manager to store JWTs, JWKS, and permissions.

Option 1: Middleware does everything (API Key + JWT + RBAC).
	sequenceDiagram
    participant Client
    participant Middleware
    participant DB as Database
    participant Cache

    Client->>Middleware: Send request with API Key + JWT
    Middleware->>DB: Verify API Key (generator/verifier)
    Middleware->>Cache: Check cached JWT/JWKS
    alt JWT not cached or expired
        Middleware->>DB: Validate JWT, roles, permissions
        Middleware->>Cache: Store JWT/JWKS with TTL
    end
    Middleware->>DB: Enforce allowlist (ContractAddress, WalletAddress)
    Middleware-->>Client: Return response (authorized or denied)


•	Option 2: API Gateway manages API Key, Middleware handles JWT + RBAC.
sequenceDiagram
    participant Client
    participant APIGW as API Gateway
    participant Middleware
    participant DB as Database
    participant Cache

    Client->>APIGW: Send request with API Key
    APIGW->>APIGW: Verify API Key (issuer/verifier)
    APIGW->>Middleware: Forward request with JWT
    Middleware->>Cache: Check cached JWT/JWKS
    alt JWT not cached or expired
        Middleware->>DB: Validate JWT, roles, permissions
        Middleware->>Cache: Store JWT/JWKS with TTL
    end
    Middleware->>DB: Enforce allowlist (ContractAddress, WalletAddress)
    Middleware-->>APIGW: Response (authorized/denied)
    APIGW-->>Client: Return response

	•	Option 3: API Gateway + IdP manage API Key + JWT, Middleware only enforces RBAC/allowlist.
sequenceDiagram
    participant Client
    participant APIGW as API Gateway
    participant IdP
    participant Middleware
    participant DB as Database
    participant Cache

    Client->>APIGW: Send request with API Key
    APIGW->>APIGW: Verify API Key (issuer/verifier)
    APIGW->>IdP: Request JWT (authentication/authorization)
    IdP->>APIGW: Return JWT
    APIGW->>Middleware: Forward request with JWT
    Middleware->>Cache: Check cached JWT/JWKS
    alt JWT not cached or expired
        Middleware->>IdP: Retrieve JWKS
        Middleware->>DB: Validate roles, permissions
        Middleware->>Cache: Store JWT/JWKS with TTL
    end
    Middleware->>DB: Enforce allowlist (ContractAddress, WalletAddress)
    Middleware-->>APIGW: Response (authorized/denied)
    APIGW-->>Client: Return response



